WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.440
Film is an expensive and complicated medium.

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:07.800
When storytellers suddenly realize that their
movie is heading for an unsatisfying conclusion,

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:12.880
it can result in some exhausting and expensive
behind-the-scenes headaches for everyone involved.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.880
Thankfully, these beloved movies swapped out
their iffy endings just in time… for the

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:17.920
most part.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.060
At the beginning of Scott Pilgrim vs. the
World, the eponymous hero is caught between

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:28.200
two women: his "too young for him" girlfriend
Knives Chau and the mysterious Ramona Flowers.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:32.439
Early on in the film, he cheats on Knives
with Ramona, and then breaks up with Knives

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:36.100
altogether, but his new relationship with
Ramona is far from smooth.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:39.180
"If we're gonna date, you may have to defeat
my seven evil exes."

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:40.560
"You have seven evil ex-boyfriends?"

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:41.940
"Seven evil exes, yes."

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:43.260
How does the story end?

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.960
Well, when Edgar Wright and Michael Bacall
were writing the script for Scott Pilgrim

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:49.400
vs the World, they had a unique problem.

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:53.960
It was based on a six-part comic series by
Bryan Lee O'Malley, but only five of the six

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:55.440
books had been released at the time.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:57.220
There was no ending for them to adapt.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.840
The initial ending they penned had Scott realizing
that Ramona wasn't right for him, and so he

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:03.059
and Knives got back together.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:06.380
This ending was shot and appears as a bonus
feature on the Blu-Ray.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:09.560
"Well, bye and stuff."

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:11.380
"Yeah.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:12.800
And stuff."

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.960
But something about it didn't feel right during
filming.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:20.760
In addition to test audiences not liking it,
one of the loudest voices against it was Ellen

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.960
Wong, the actor playing Knives, who didn't
want her character going back to a man who

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:25.960
cheated on her.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.490
In the final ending, despite their problems,
Scott and Ramona decide that they're going

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:32.580
to try to make it work, and Knives learns
to stand on her own.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:34.100
"Bye and stuff."

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:36.180
"Yeah.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:38.640
And stuff."

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:41.320
"Go get her."

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.900
This ended up matching the resolution of the
comic series, the last volume of which was

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.440
released less than a month before the film
hit theaters.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:54.470
Rogue One: A Star Wars Story was a project
that was constantly shifting around during

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:55.780
all stages of its production.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.780
There was an early version of the script where
protagonist Jyn Erso was a Sergeant an enlisted

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:04.840
soldier in the Rebel Alliance instead of a
criminal who was pressured into joining the

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:05.380
rebellion.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:10.160
In some versions of the script, she and Cassian
actually survived the battle on Scarif.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:14.080
This incarnation of Jyn was the one that the
filmmakers thought they would be going with

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.180
when much of the merchandise was created.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:20.180
Because of that, some of the packaging for
the toys still refers to Jyn as Sergeant Jyn

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:20.680
Erso.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:25.099
However, there was another key change to the
ending that was made very late into the filming

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:25.600
process.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.940
While looking over the footage they had filmed
for the climax, director Gareth Edwards and

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:35.080
editor Jabez Olssen realized that Darth Vader,
despite being introduced in an early scene,

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:37.740
never really got a chance to do anything cool
in the third act.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:42.450
They decided he needed an action scene of
his own, and so the now-iconic Darth Vader

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:46.260
hallway fight scene was shot just four months
before the film was released.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.959
As a filmmaker, Terry Gilliam is known for
two things: making amazing, surreal movies

00:02:51.959 --> 00:02:55.760
and having terrible luck while trying to make
amazing, surreal movies.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:00.319
The story of his 1985 film Brazil showcases
both of these traits.

00:03:00.319 --> 00:03:05.500
Brazil tells the story of Sam Lowry, who lives
in a totalitarian futuristic bureaucracy but

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:09.260
dreams of being a winged hero who fights monsters
and rescues damsels.

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:14.460
After a series of misadventures, Sam is captured
by the authorities for associating with criminal

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.340
revolutionaries.

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:20.020
In the original ending, he seemingly breaks
out of captivity and flees the city to an

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:21.380
idyllic countryside.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:24.400
But then, there is a sudden reveal that he
hasn't fled at all.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:28.240
He is still in prison, merely dreaming of
escape after being lobotomized.

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:32.900
The Criterion documentary The Battle of Brazil
tells the story of what happened next.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:36.080
Distributors felt that U.S. audiences would
want a happier ending.

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:39.280
"I would get calls from the editor occasionally
wanting my input.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:42.160
What do you mean, you want my input?

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:43.970
'You know, but we're saving your film.'

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:46.000
No, no, no — you're killing my film."

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:51.140
Against Gilliam's wishes, an editing team
created an alternate cut that ended with Sam

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:54.440
actually escaping, forgoing the reveal that
it was just a hallucination.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:58.510
This was the version of Brazil that originally
premiered in the United States.

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:00.520
Fortunately, this is not the end of the story.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.000
Gilliam, unhappy with this result, starting
hosting private screenings of his version

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:05.700
of the film.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:10.200
His cut started getting positive critical
attention, eventually winning the Los Angeles

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.380
Film Critics Award for Best Picture.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:16.880
This made the studio finally cave and release
the original version to American audiences.

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:22.260
When Sean Harris was cast in Mission: Impossible
- Rogue Nation as the villainous Solomon Lane,

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:26.240
he had only one request, without which he
would not agree to do the film.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:28.380
He said, quote, "Promise me you'll kill him…

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:30.640
I don't want to be in five of these movies."

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:33.380
Writer/director Christopher McQuarrie agreed.

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:36.870
By the end of the film, protagonist Ethan
Hunt would kill Solomon Lane.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:37.460
The problem?

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.480
Late into process of filming, McQuarrie made
an upsetting discovery.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:45.960
IndieWire reports that the writer/director
admitted, quote, "I [didn't] have the event

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:48.100
in the movie that [made] me want Ethan to
kill him."

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:53.520
In fact, McQuarrie felt it would be more satisfying
if Ethan tricked Lane, defeating him without

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.860
violence, and brought him into custody alive.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:05:00.080
After some pleading, McQuarrie eventually
got Harris to agree to a script change.

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:02.389
In the end, Harris, like his character, was
trapped.

00:05:02.389 --> 00:05:06.440
It hasn't been five films yet, but Solomon
Lane did indeed have to return in Mission:

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:07.860
Impossible - Fallout.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:13.390
Pretty in Pink tells the story of Andie Walsh
played by Molly Ringwald a highschooler caught

00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:17.910
between two love interests: the preppy hunk
Blaine and Duckie, the boy next door.

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:21.680
Originally, the plan was for Andie to learn
that people who are rich and attractive like

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.030
Blaine can sometimes be shallow, and people
who don't look like much are often the real

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:26.520
treasure.

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:28.500
That's, of course, Duckie we're talking about.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:33.700
However, once filming began, Ringwald and
McCarthy ended up having undeniable chemistry.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:39.240
Duckie, meanwhile, came off as a deeply weird
dude with strange fashion sense, odd mannerisms,

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:42.140
and an almost stalker-esque obsession with
Andie.

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:46.259
The film was shot as originally written, with
Andie ending up with Duckie, but audiences

00:05:46.259 --> 00:05:49.550
who were shown early cuts of the film just
about lost their minds.

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:51.190
As director Howard Deutch put it:

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:54.750
"The girls in the test screening didn't go
for that… they wanted her to get the cute

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:55.240
boy.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:55.820
And that was it.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:57.480
So we had to reshoot the ending."

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:02.260
The final version of the film did indeed have
Andie ending up with Blaine, but the change

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:06.460
happened so late into the process that the
novelization of the film still has the original

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:06.960
ending.

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:09.820
This change never sat well with writer John
Hughes.

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:14.139
Wanting another chance to properly execute
this story, he wrote Some Kind of Wonderful,

00:06:14.139 --> 00:06:18.740
which has a very similar setup to Pretty in
Pink, but this time ends with the lead choosing

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:20.160
the normie over the hottie.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:21.320
Realism be damned!

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:26.900
World War Z's original ending, which was actually
filmed, was an enormous and expensive action

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:31.590
set piece, with protagonist Gerry Lane played
by Brad Pitt fighting his way through hundreds

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:33.480
of zombies in Moscow's Red Square.

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:37.680
However, when watching it back, the filmmakers
felt that it just wasn't working.

00:06:37.690 --> 00:06:42.080
The story began with a strong focus on Gerry's
desire to reunite with his family, but it

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:45.300
ended with a meaningless bloodbath and no
resolution to his main goal.

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:49.900
In an interview with Vanity Fair, second unit
director Simon Crane said that the biggest

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:52.930
problem was that, quote, "it wasn't character-driven
anymore."

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:57.639
Writer Damon Lindelof was brought on to take
things back to the drawing board and do something

00:06:57.639 --> 00:07:02.070
that had never been done before on a film
of this scale: write a completely new final

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:04.520
40 minutes to an already finished film.

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:08.970
In the version that hit theaters, the new
climax is confined to an abandoned hospital.

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:13.680
The action is small, with Gerry as a frightened
everyman, rather than a swaggering badass.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:17.520
Most importantly, in the end, he's finally
reunited with his family.

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:21.880
Even though Ridley Scott's Blade Runner is
considered a classic nowadays, it was not

00:07:21.889 --> 00:07:24.100
always a favorite of sci fi cinephiles.

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:28.300
When it premiered, reviews were lukewarm,
and some last minute studio meddling may have

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:29.300
been to blame.

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:33.940
In Scott's initial cut, the storytelling was
gloomy and mysterious, especially the last

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:38.030
shot of the film, which showed our protagonists
on the run from the law, boarding an elevator

00:07:38.030 --> 00:07:39.300
to an uncertain fate.

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:43.720
According to the making-of documentary On
the Edge of Blade Runner, initial test screenings

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:48.460
indicated that this highly ambiguous plot
did its job a little too well, because everyone

00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:50.230
who saw it felt highly confused.

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:52.780
"What the f--- am I watching here?"

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:57.090
The studio pressured Scott to add not only
a voiceover narration to make things clearer,

00:07:57.090 --> 00:08:01.500
but also a new ending that showed the heroes
driving through the sunny countryside, indicating

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:03.630
that they'd found some semblance of happiness.

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:07.530
The production was out of money, but Scott
was suddenly in need of some idyllic landscape

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:08.220
footage.

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:12.300
He turned to his friend, Stanley Kubrick,
who happened to have 17 hours of beautiful

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:16.620
unused b-roll that he originally shot for
the opening titles of The Shining.

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:21.220
Despite everyone's best efforts, the fix ended
up pleasing exactly nobody.

00:08:21.229 --> 00:08:25.169
Some still thought the movie was confusing,
while others thought the new voiceover and

00:08:25.169 --> 00:08:26.260
ending were too corny.

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:30.960
It wasn't until 1992 that Ridley Scott released
a version of the film more in line with his

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:35.270
initial vision, and lovers of confusing gloom
finally got the flick they'd been dreaming

00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:35.760
of.

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:41.080
Speaking of Stanley Kubrick, the notoriously
intense director is no stranger to switching

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:43.149
things up at the last possible minute.

00:08:43.149 --> 00:08:45.160
How "last minute" are we talking, exactly?

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:48.720
Well, he changed the ending to The Shining
a week after it came out!

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:52.709
The initial ending of the film featured an
additional scene between the two now-famous

00:08:52.709 --> 00:08:57.000
final beats of Jack freezing to death in the
hedge maze and the closing shot of that spooky

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:59.020
group photo back in the Overlook.

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:04.110
This diversion showed Wendy and Danny recovering
in a hospital and talking to the police about

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:05.240
what they experienced.

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:09.680
This scene was actually shown at the premiere
and in the film's initial limited run in Los

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:10.920
Angeles and New York.

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:15.300
Then, before the wider release of the film,
Kubrick decided the scene was superfluous

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:20.230
and sent out assistants to actually travel
around to dozens of theaters and physically

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:24.180
cut out the scene, re-editing the film reels
that the theaters had been showing.

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:28.440
The scene is not currently available anywhere,
since all copies of it were theoretically

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:28.940
destroyed.

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:33.320
Only a few set photos still exist, but the
evidence of how late this change occurred

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:37.710
can be seen in that two characters from that
scene, a "Policeman" and a "Nurse," still

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:41.260
appear in the final credits, despite no longer
being in the movie.

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:45.900
So, in summation: Blade Runner's team took
footage from The Shining to create a new ending,

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:50.000
while The Shining crew also crafted a new
ending for the horror film by racing around

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:57.280
the country and taking razor blades to films,
effectively becoming — pardon the pun — "blade

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:57.780
runners."

00:09:58.460 --> 00:09:58.960
"Oh!

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:00.600
I get it."

00:10:01.990 --> 00:10:06.410
The classic '90s slacker comedy Clerks tells
the story of a day in the life of sad sack

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:09.000
convenience store clerk Dante Hicks.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:13.110
Even though the film garnered critical acclaim,
jump-started the career of director Kevin

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:17.800
Smith, and introduced the world to Jay and
Silent Bob, the original ending told a drastically

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:18.800
different story.

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:23.420
In its first public screening at the Independent
Feature Film Market, Clerks ended with one

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:26.880
last customer entering the store as Dante
was closing up for the night.

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:32.260
He was an armed robber who, without warning
or fanfare, shot Dante dead, emptied the register,

00:10:32.260 --> 00:10:33.300
and walked away.

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:34.600
Talk about a dead end job!

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:39.029
In the making-of documentary Snowball Effect:
The Story of Clerks, Smith admits that he

00:10:39.029 --> 00:10:42.470
did this because he didn't know how else to
close the film, and because he was copying

00:10:42.470 --> 00:10:46.410
the structure of Spike Lee's Do the Right
Thing, which also takes place entirely in

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:48.540
one day and ends in tragedy.

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:53.990
Brian O'Halloran, the actor who played Dante,
hated this ending, as did Smith's mentor,

00:10:53.990 --> 00:10:55.040
filmmaker John Pierson.

00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:58.680
Pierson suggested simply cutting that scene
and ending on the happy note that preceded

00:10:58.680 --> 00:10:59.180
it.

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:03.360
That's exactly what Smith did, and so all
future screenings ended in a way that better

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:07.020
fit the tone of the rest of the film, and
left the door open for a sequel.

